<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeolla Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeolla Province was one of the Eight Provinces of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in the fertile south-west of the peninsula, divided in 1896 into North and South Jeolla. Once part of the Mahan confederacy and then Baekje, it was the base of Later Baekje in the 10th century and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution</a> in 1894. Its name joins those of its two main towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naju">Naju</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeolla-province.jpg" alt="Jeolla Province" width="320" height="520" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 고려 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeolla_Province_of_Late_Joseon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jeolla was bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chungcheong-province">Chungcheong Province</a> to the north and Gyeongsang Province to the east, with the East China Sea to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> to the west. The Sobaek Mountains form its eastern edge, and it is drained by the Yeongsan, Seomjin and Mangyeong rivers. Its provincial capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a>, and the whole inland region was called Honam, south of the lake, a name still in common use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Samhan era the area belonged to the Mahan confederacy, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timna-yemen">Tamna</a> kingdom on Jeju; fifteen of the 45 Korean tribes had their bases here. Baekje took over Mahan by the 5th century. After Silla conquered Baekje with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in 660, the region was organised as the districts of Wansan, later renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a>, and Namwon in the north and Mujin, renamed Mu in 757, in the south.</p><p>Goryeo reorganised the country several times. In 983 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a> became Jeonju-mok and Muju was split into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naju">Naju</a> and Seungju; in 995 these became Gangnam-do and Haeyang-do. In 1018, in the ninth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyeonjong-of-goryeo">Hyeonjong of Goryeo</a>, the two were joined as Jeollaju, named after Jeonju and Naju. It was the first time the region was united in one division, and it stayed so for nearly eight centuries. Joseon kept it among the Eight Provinces set up in 1413, and the name was shortened to Jeolla-do.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution</a> of 1894-1895 began in Jeolla, fed by belief in a coming local messiah, protests at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>'s high taxes on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and the growing number of Japanese traders. On 26 May 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Gojong of Korea</a> replaced the provinces with 23 districts, and on 4 August 1896 Royal Order 36 restored the provinces and divided Jeolla into north and south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old province corresponds to the South Korean provinces of North Jeolla and South Jeolla, the city of Gwangju and Jeju Province. Its largest city is Gwangju; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a> remains the capital of North Jeolla. The name Jeolla-do reached its thousandth year in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeolla Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is performed mostly in South <b>Jeolla Province</b> on the south-western coast, at Chuseok and at Daeboreum, when the full moon rises above the mountains, and also at New Year and Dano.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ganggangsullae">Ganggangsullae</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Baekje controlled the fertile <b>Jeolla</b> region, maintained diplomatic relations with the Wuyue kingdom and Later Tang in China, and fielded strong armies.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-baekje">Later Baekje</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahan took shape around the 1st century BCE in what are now Gyeonggi, Chungcheong and <b>Jeolla</b> provinces.</blockquote><a href="/state/mahan">Mahan</a></li></ul></section>
